{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by rising Islamic power under Saladin. His early life involved captivity following the Battle of Inab yet he eventually secured his freedom and assumed control over his ancestral lands in northern Syria. Raymond established himself as a pragmatic leader who often favored diplomacy over immediate military confrontation with neighboring Muslim states. He understood the fragile nature of Crusader holdings and sought alliances that would ensure survival rather than reckless expansion.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nThe young count faced immense hardship after his father died in battle against Nur ad-Din. Raymond spent roughly ten years as a prisoner in Aleppo where he learned Arabic and gained deep insight into Islamic culture and politics. This period of confinement shaped his worldview and made him distinct among Latin nobility because he appreciated negotiation tactics used by Eastern rulers. His release came through a ransom payment organized by the Byzantine Empire and this event allowed him to return to Tripoli to reclaim his title and restore order within his county.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nRaymond acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule without assistance. During this time he navigated complex court factions including the aggressive policies of Guy of Lusignan and the Templar Order. He opposed hasty wars that might provoke Saladin into full-scale invasion and his caution often put him at odds with more militant barons. The internal discord weakened the kingdom significantly and prevented unified responses to external threats while Raymond tried to maintain stability through careful diplomatic channels.\n\n*Battle of Hattin and Legacy*\nT